@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# OCI snapshot references
+
+The OCI storage backend implements `ReferencedSnapshotStore`. Storage requests
+forward their reference to it. An empty reference reads the resident configured
+default, initialized before construction succeeds and refreshed by polling.
+A repository configured without a tag uses `latest`.
+
+An explicit reference is either an OCI tag (up to 128 ASCII characters, starting
+with a letter, digit, or underscore) or `sha256:` followed by 64 lowercase hex
+digits. Repository paths, registry names, schemes, queries, and fragments are
+rejected. A request cannot change the configured repository or its authentication.
+
+An explicit tag resolves when it starts a new acquisition. Overlapping callers
+for the same reference share that acquisition. A completed acquisition does not
+make a tag immutable: the next explicit request resolves it again. Tags have
+independent bindings even when they currently point at the same manifest.
+
+A digest addresses the complete registry manifest, including its annotations.
+`internal/oci.FetchResponse.ManifestDigest` records that identity.
+`FetchResponse.Digest` remains the annotation-insensitive content change detector
+used by `IfNoMatch`; it must never be used as a registry address. Annotations alone
+can change the manifest identity without requiring a new parsed snapshot.
+
+The store retains the default and at most three other reference entries, using
+recent acquisition order for eviction. A digest request can reuse a retained
+snapshot verified against that manifest digest. There is no additional unbounded
+cache of parsed snapshots. ORAS's underlying verified blob storage is independent
+of this reference-entry bound. Entries can be evicted while a callback holds a
+snapshot; the callback keeps its own reference and remains valid.
+
+All files in a candidate bundle must parse and validate before publication.
+Failed polling preserves the default's last complete good snapshot. A failed
+explicit tag acquisition returns its error, including when an older snapshot is
+cached. Another reference is unaffected. A later successful acquisition recovers
+normally. At most one uncancelled acquisition exists per reference; publication
+and removal from the in-flight map occur together. Abandoned work is checked for
+cancellation and ownership before it can publish.
+
+Acquisition work uses the store's lifetime context. Cancelling a caller before or
+during acquisition returns that caller's context error without running its
+callback. Cancelling an initiator or waiter does not cancel work needed by another
+waiter. If all callers depart, the acquisition is cancelled; later callers can
+start fresh work. Network operations and callbacks hold no store mutex, so a
+blocked reference cannot prevent a resident default, cached digest, or unrelated
+reference from making progress.
+
+`SnapshotStore.Close` is concurrent and repeat safe. It cancels and joins
+acquisition work and stops polling, without waiting for user callbacks. A View
+admitted before Close owns its callback lifetime and stable snapshot. Later Views
+return `oci.ErrClosed`; an acquiring View returns that error or its own context
+error. Parent-context cancellation also ends the store's lifetime. Callers should
+still call Close to join background work. No new fetch starts after Close returns.
+
+Fetch transfers file ownership only on success and closes acquired files on
+failure. Snapshot construction then owns every supplied file, closing all of them
+on success, Stat/read/validation failure, or failure in a later namespace.
+`SnapshotFromPaths` closes already opened files if another open fails. Cleanup
+errors do not replace the originating error. OCI parsing uses the verified copied
+layers instead of opening the same layers again from a mutable remote source.
+
+The factory, static/ECR authentication, OCI manifest 1.0/1.1 support, and shared
+non-OCI parsing use the existing interfaces and configuration. The old OCI View
+signature changes to include `storage.Reference`; direct callers should pass an
+empty reference to retain default behavior.
+
+## Verification
+
+The reference tests exercise tag moves, alias independence, failure atomicity,
+retention across an active callback, acquisition cancellation, remote disconnects,
+polling shutdown, and repeated Close using loopback registry barriers. Parser and
+Fetch tests instrument ownership and preserve existing content-detector behavior.
+The factory test reads distinct flag values through default and explicit requests.
+
+Run the offline packages and focused race checks with the repository's Go toolchain:
+
+```sh
+go test ./internal/config ./internal/oci/... ./internal/storage/fs ./internal/storage/fs/oci ./internal/storage/fs/store ./internal/storage/fs/local ./internal/storage/fs/git ./internal/storage/fs/object
+go test -race -count=3 ./internal/oci ./internal/storage/fs ./internal/storage/fs/oci
+```
+
+Optional Git/cloud integration cases require their documented environment
+variables and services; an offline run does not claim those service-backed cases.
